  {
   "id": "concept1",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Sequential circuits and the NOR SR latch"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "In a sequential circuit the output depends on the present inputs and on the past state, so it needs memory. The basic memory element is the latch, a bistable circuit that stores one bit. Two cross-coupled NOR gates form an SR latch: S = 1 sets Q to 1, R = 1 resets Q to 0, S = R = 0 keeps (holds) the previous Q, and S = R = 1 is not allowed because it forces Q = $\\bar{Q}$ = 0 and the state after it is unpredictable."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "Sequential output = f(inputs, previous state)"
    },
    {
     "en": "Latch: bistable, stores 1 bit"
    },
    {
     "en": "NOR latch: S = 1 sets, R = 1 resets, 00 holds, 11 invalid"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "A sequential circuit remembers its past state. Two cross-coupled NOR gates form an S R latch: set makes Q one, reset makes Q zero, both zero holds, and both one is forbidden.",
    "kn": "ಅನುಕ್ರಮ (ಸೀಕ್ವೆನ್ಶಿಯಲ್) ಮಂಡಲ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಸ್ಥಿತಿಯನ್ನು ನೆನಪಿಡುತ್ತದೆ. ಎರಡು NOR ಗೇಟ್‌ಗಳಿಂದ SR ಲ್ಯಾಚ್: S = 1 ಸೆಟ್, R = 1 ರೀಸೆಟ್, ಎರಡೂ 0 ಆದರೆ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಸ್ಥಿತಿ, ಎರಡೂ 1 ನಿಷಿದ್ಧ."
   },
   "formula": "Q_{n+1} = S + \\bar{R}\\,Q_n \\quad (S\\cdot R = 0)"
  },
